#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:262
msgid "4. Split, join and replace"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:264
msgid "`split`: a line into fields"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:273
msgid "There are two kinds of `split`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:275
msgid "`split(\",\")` with a separator: **every** comma cuts, so two commas give an **empty** field. This is right for CSV, where an empty field means a missing value."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:278
msgid "`split()` without an argument: every group of spaces, tabs and new lines cuts, and there are no empty fields. This is right for words."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:288
msgid "A second argument limits the number of cuts. `rsplit` cuts from the right - the last word of a company name is its legal form:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:300
msgid "`partition` cuts at the **first** separator and always gives three parts: the text before, the separator, the text after. It is handy for `key=value`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:311
msgid "(The name `_` is a normal variable. By convention it means \"I do not need this value\".)"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:314
msgid "`join`: fields into a line"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:316
msgid "`separator.join(list_of_strings)` - the separator comes **first**, the list goes in the brackets:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:327
msgid "This is `CATX(',', a, b, c)` in SAS, and `CONCAT_WS(',', ...)` or `STRING_AGG` in SQL."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:329
msgid "`join` takes only strings"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:330
msgid "A number in the list is a `TypeError`. Convert the values first:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:339
msgid "Do not use `split(\",\")` to read a CSV file"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:340
msgid "A field can contain a comma, inside quotes: `C0026,\"Silver Textiles, Plovdiv\",AD`. `split` does not know quotes; the `csv` module does. That is why the examples since [session 6](./06-dictionaries.md#a-row-as-a-dictionary) read files with `csv.DictReader`. [Lecture 8](./08-errors-and-files.md#_7-csv-files) covers the `csv` module."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:347
msgid "`replace(old, new)` replaces **every** occurrence. A third argument limits the number:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:357
msgid "Several `replace` calls in a row run from left to right - each one works on the result of the one before:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:366
msgid "`removeprefix` and `removesuffix`"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:368
msgid "They remove a text at the start or at the end - only if it is there:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:377
msgid "This is not the same as `strip(\"gr. \")`. `strip` removes the **characters** g, r, `.` and space from both ends, as long as it finds them:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:385
msgid "`zfill`: zeros in front"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:387
msgid "`zfill` adds zeros on the left until the text has the given length - the SAS format `Z`:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:397
msgid "The same in pandas"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:398
msgid "In pandas, every string method works on a whole column with `.str`: `loans[\"product\"].str.upper()`, `names.str.split()`, `ids.str.startswith(\"L\")` ([lecture 14](./14-pandas-select-aggregate-join.md#text-and-dates-in-a-column)):"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:411
msgid "`settings = \"lgd=0.45;currency=EUR;report_date=2026-09-30\"`. Turn it into a dictionary `{\"lgd\": \"0.45\", ...}`: `split(\";\")` gives the three parts, and `partition(\"=\")` cuts each part into a key and a value. Then join the dictionary back into the same string."
msgstr ""

