#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:579
msgid "`float()` understands only one format: a point for the decimals, and no separators between the thousands. Text from Excel, from reports and from people has other formats:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:589
msgid "When you know the format of the source, bring the text to the Python format with `replace`, then convert. The Bulgarian format has spaces between the thousands and a decimal comma:"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:602
msgid "Know the format of the source"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:603
msgid "`\"1,234\"` - is it one thousand two hundred thirty-four (the English format), or one point two three four (the Bulgarian format)? The text does not say. **Ask** which format a source uses and write one function for it. Do not guess value by value. (A column with several formats, like `turnover` in the register: see [Numbers in any format](#numbers-in-any-format) in the Self-study part.)"
msgstr ""

