#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1494
msgid "A file on disk is **bytes**, not text. An **encoding** is the rule that turns text into bytes and back. The same text gives different bytes in different encodings:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1507
msgid "**UTF-8** - every character of every language; the standard of the web, of Python source files and of most new files."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1509
msgid "**cp1251** (Windows-1251) - the old Windows encoding for Cyrillic. Exports from old systems and Excel files saved as \"CSV\" on a Bulgarian Windows are often cp1251."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1512
msgid "Reading with the wrong encoding gives either an error, or **mojibake** - wrong letters:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1523
msgid "That is why every `open()` in this course says `encoding=\"utf-8\"`: without it, Python 3.12 - 3.14 on Windows uses the Windows encoding of the computer (often cp1252 or cp1251), and the same script gives different results on different computers. (From Python 3.15 on, UTF-8 is the default everywhere. Still write it: the script may run on an older Python.)"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1529
msgid "The file `regions_bg.csv` is saved in **cp1251**:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1539
msgid "`errors=\"replace\"` puts `?` (or `�`) instead of a byte that cannot be read, instead of stopping. Use it only to **look** at a file, never to load data."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/07-text-and-regex.md:1542
msgid "One more detail: Excel saves \"CSV UTF-8\" with a **BOM** - three bytes `EF BB BF` at the start. Read such a file with `encoding=\"utf-8-sig\"`, or the first column name gets an invisible character in front:"
